{"type":"llm_response","timestamp":"2026-04-08T07:55:12.182Z","data":{"turn":61,"content":"{\"type\":\"thinking\",\"thinking\":\"Looking at Conversation.jsx:\\n- Line 184: `src={other?.avatar_url || ...}` - avatar_url from server is placed in a `src` attribute. This could potentially be exploited if the value contains `javascript:` - but normally browsers don't execute `javascript:` URLs in `src` attributes of img tags. Let me check this further.\\n- Line 235: `{msg.content && <p className=\\\"chat-bubble-text\\\">{msg.content}</p>}` - This uses React's built-in JSX rendering which HTML-escapes the content, so it's safe.\\n\\n\\n- Line 238-240: The attachment URL is being used directly in an href attribute without validation, which means if an attacker controls that value they could inject a `javascript:` URL to execute arbitrary code when clicked. I need to check how the attachment_url gets set in the database and whether there's any sanitization happening upstream.\\n\\nSimilarly, line 116 has the same vulnerability pattern with `deliverable_url` in the contracts service. Let me trace both of these back to their sources to understand the attack surface.\\n\\nI'm also noticing the SVG data URI construction on lines 184 and 227 that's encoding SVG markup...\\n\\nGenerating SVG data URIs...\",\"signature\":\"\"}","timestamp":"2026-04-08T07:55:12.182Z"}}
