{"type":"llm_response","timestamp":"2026-04-08T07:59:23.653Z","data":{"turn":77,"content":"{\"type\":\"thinking\",\"thinking\":\"Now I see the messaging system. Let me look at how `attachment_url` is set in the response and compare it to what the frontend renders.\\n\\nLooking at the `sendMessage` in the controller (line 67-74), attachments are objects with:\\n```js\\n{\\n  filename: f.originalname,\\n  path: '/uploads/' + f.filename,\\n  size: f.size,\\n  mimetype: f.mimetype\\n}\\n```\\n\\nSo `attachment_url` in the frontend renders based on this `path` field. This is a file path to `/uploads/<uuid><ext>`.\\n\\nBut wait - the frontend is looking for `msg.attachment_url` (line 238 in Conversation.jsx), but the backend stores attachments as JSON array in the `attachments` column, with `path` as the field name. \\n\\nI need to trace how messages are retrieved from the GET endpoint to see if `attachment_url` and `attachment_name` are being constructed or mapped from the stored `attachments` JSON structure.\\n\\nLooking at the Conversation.jsx component, I can see the attachment link is rendered directly from `msg.attachment_url`, which could be vulnerable to javascript: URLs. The real concern though is the stored XSS vector from file uploads—when users upload HTML files, they're stored at `/uploads/<uuid>.html` and served directly by express.static, which means the browser will execute any scripts in that HTML file. I need to trace where `attachment_url` comes from when messages are retrieved to see if user input can flow into that field.\",\"signature\":\"\"}","timestamp":"2026-04-08T07:59:23.652Z"}}
{"type":"llm_response","timestamp":"2026-04-08T07:59:23.983Z","data":{"turn":78,"content":"{\"type\":\"tool_use\",\"id\":\"toolu_01NA6o1AKBqiprvoCtUkGDfw\",\"name\":\"Read\",\"input\":{\"file_path\":\"/repos/webvulnbench/src/messaging/messaging.service.js\",\"limit\":80,\"offset\":180},\"caller\":{\"type\":\"direct\"}}","timestamp":"2026-04-08T07:59:23.983Z"}}
