{"type":"llm_response","timestamp":"2026-04-08T08:05:27.077Z","data":{"turn":151,"content":"{\"type\":\"thinking\",\"thinking\":\"Now I have the playwright-cli available. Let me use it to do browser-based live XSS testing. My plan:\\n\\n1. Open the browser and navigate to the app\\n2. Register as a client and a freelancer\\n3. Create the necessary data flow (project → proposal → contract → review)\\n4. Submit an XSS payload in the review comment\\n5. Visit the gig page as another user to confirm execution\\n\\nActually, let me think of a simpler approach. Instead of creating the full flow through the UI, let me:\\n\\n1. Check what gigs already exist on the platform (the API showed empty, but maybe the UI shows something different)\\n\\n\\n2. If no gigs exist, create one through the UI as a freelancer\\n3. Create a contract using the API (if possible) or through the UI\\n\\nI realize the review contract validation only checks that a contract exists, not whether the reviewer is actually party to it. So I can create a project as a client, have a freelancer submit a proposal, accept it to generate a contract, and then inject the XSS payload in the review comment.\\n\\nFor the live XSS test, I'll use Playwright to walk through the full flow: register both accounts, create a gig, post a project, submit and accept a proposal to establish the contract, then post a review with the payload and verify it executes on the gig page.\\n\\nActually, let me start by checking if there's an existing gig I can use to simplify this.\",\"signature\":\"\"}","timestamp":"2026-04-08T08:05:27.077Z"}}
