{"type":"llm_response","timestamp":"2026-04-08T08:08:37.935Z","data":{"turn":193,"content":"{\"type\":\"thinking\",\"thinking\":\"The `create` function does `new Gig(data)` and saves directly. The issue is likely with how the data is being processed. \\n\\nThe controller code does:\\n```js\\nconst gigData = {\\n  ...req.body,  // includes all form fields\\n  freelancer_id: req.user.id,\\n  images\\n};\\n\\nif (typeof gigData.pricing_tiers === 'string') {\\n  gigData.pricing_tiers = JSON.parse(gigData.pricing_tiers);\\n\\n\\n```\\n\\nBut the client sends `pricing` not `pricing_tiers`. So `pricing_tiers` would be undefined. And `pricing` would be the JSON string.\\n\\nLooking at the client code:\\n```js\\nfd.append('pricing', JSON.stringify(pricingPayload));\\n```\\n\\nThe controller looks for `pricing_tiers` not `pricing`. So the pricing from the client never gets processed.\\n\\nAlso, there seem to be extra fields being added. The error shows `tags.6`, `images.6`, `requirements.6`. This is suspicious - like there are 7 values\\n\\nThe controller is also parsing tags and pricing_tiers if they're strings, but there's a mismatch between what the client sends and what the controller expects. The client sends `pricing` while the controller checks for `pricing_tiers`, and those extra indexed fields in the error suggest the data structure isn't being handled correctly somewhere. I'm realizing the issue might be that `req.body.images` is being populated with a stringified empty array from the form data, and when I spread it alongside the actual images array from multer, it's creating a conflict that causes the validation error. Let me trace through what's actually happening on the client side to see if something's being sent that shouldn't be. Looking at the curl command, I'm sending `requirements=[]` and `faqs=[]` as string values, which Multer puts into `req.body`. The controller then parses these strings back into arrays, which should work fine. But I'm still puzzled about why `tags.6` is showing up as `\\\"[]\\\"` — that suggests something in the multer configuration or how the arrays are being processed is treating the string `\\\"[]\\\"` as individual characters and spreading them across array indices.\\n\\nLet me step back and test this using the browser's fetch API instead, sending the form data exactly as the React app would, to see if the issue is specific to how I'm constructing the curl request.\",\"signature\":\"\"}","timestamp":"2026-04-08T08:08:37.935Z"}}
{"type":"llm_response","timestamp":"2026-04-08T08:08:42.131Z","data":{"turn":194,"content":"{\"type\":\"tool_use\",\"id\":\"toolu_0157w1o1yHbfJtZAL3FMgARN\",\"name\":\"Bash\",\"input\":{\"command\":\"# Use browser to call the API with exact same format as React app\\nplaywright-cli -s=agent2 run-code \\\"async page => {\\n  const result = await page.evaluate(async () => {\\n    const fd = new FormData();\\n    fd.append('title', 'Professional Web Development Services');\\n    fd.append('category', 'web-development');\\n    fd.append('description', 'Professional web development services including React, Node.js, database design and API development for modern applications.');\\n    fd.append('tags', JSON.stringify(['web-development', 'nodejs', 'react']));\\n    const pricing = {\\n      basic: {\\n        price: 50,\\n        delivery_days: 7,\\n        description: 'Complete web dev package',\\n        features: ['React', 'Node.js'],\\n        revisions: 3\\n      }\\n    };\\n    fd.append('pricing', JSON.stringify(pricing));\\n    \\n    const resp = await fetch('/api/gigs', {\\n      method: 'POST',\\n      body: fd,\\n      credentials: 'include'\\n    });\\n    const data = await resp.json();\\n    return JSON.stringify(data);\\n  });\\n  return result;\\n}\\\" 2>&1\",\"description\":\"Create gig via browser fetch API\"},\"caller\":{\"type\":\"direct\"}}","timestamp":"2026-04-08T08:08:42.131Z"}}
{"type":"llm_response","timestamp":"2026-04-08T08:08:51.252Z","data":{"turn":195,"content":"{\"type\":\"thinking\",\"thinking\":\"The same error. The problem is persistent - there are `tags.6`, `images.6`, `requirements.6` errors. \\n\\nLooking at the error: `\\\"tags.6\\\": Cast to [undefined] failed for value \\\"[]\\\"` - this suggests the 7th element of the tags array is \\\"[]\\\" (a string).\\n\\nThe issue is that when I send `tags=JSON.stringify(['web-development', 'nodejs', 'react'])`, the controller does `JSON.parse(gigData.tags)` which gives `['web-development', 'nodejs', 'react']`. But where does the extra `.6` element come from?\\n \\nI'm wondering if the multer configuration is the culprit here - specifically how it handles the form data when no images are uploaded. The `.6` index appearing across multiple fields suggests something systematic is happening during the parsing or form submission process.\",\"signature\":\"\"}","timestamp":"2026-04-08T08:08:51.252Z"}}
