{"type":"llm_response","timestamp":"2026-04-08T08:11:50.703Z","data":{"turn":219,"content":"{\"type\":\"thinking\",\"thinking\":\"The server is returning 400 (Bad Request) for the gig creation. The validation error is happening server-side. There's a persistent issue with the gig creation API.\\n\\nLet me try a completely different approach. Instead of trying to fix the gig creation, let me:\\n1. Use the MongoDB to create a gig directly - but I can't access MongoDB directly\\n2. Use the existing users who already have gigs (from the reviews/contracts data)\\n\\nWait - actually the review data shows reviews for freelancer `d9d272cc-4ab8-4ffc-a60f-93108836bed4`. Let me check if this user has any gigs via the user gigs endpoint.\\n\\n\\nThe endpoint returned empty when I checked that freelancer's gigs. I'm realizing the gig search only returns active gigs, so there might be gigs in other statuses that aren't showing up. Since I can't access MongoDB directly, I need to find another way to work with the existing data or modify the gig status through the API.\\n\\nActually, let me step back. The GigDetail page renders reviews with `dangerouslySetInnerHTML`, and I've already verified the XSS payload is stored in the database and returned unescaped by the API. The real blocker is that I need an active gig to navigate to a GigDetail page and trigger the payload. I could try creating a gig, but I'm hitting validation errors with the tags field—it's rejecting the 7th element as an empty string when it should be accepting my input. I'm realizing the issue might be that `req.body.tags` is already arriving as an array of strings rather than a JSON string, which would skip the `JSON.parse` step entirely and leave `gigData.tags` with an extra element somehow. Let me trace back where that 7th element with value `\\\"[]\\\"` is actually coming from in the request body. I'm wondering if multer might be parsing the JSON string differently depending on its version or configuration—maybe it's treating the stringified array as separate form fields instead of a single value, which would explain why the tags are getting spread out oddly. Let me check what version of multer is being used to understand how it's handling the form data.\",\"signature\":\"\"}","timestamp":"2026-04-08T08:11:50.703Z"}}
