{"type":"llm_response","timestamp":"2026-04-08T08:12:44.359Z","data":{"turn":230,"content":"{\"type\":\"thinking\",\"thinking\":\"I can't find MongoDB/mongoose packages accessible from the host filesystem. The node_modules are inside the Docker container.\\n\\nLet me try a completely different approach. Since I can connect to MongoDB on port 27017, let me try to send a raw MongoDB wire protocol message to insert a gig directly. MongoDB uses a binary wire protocol, but I could construct the message.\\n\\nActually, that's overly complex. Let me think of another way.\\n\\nAlternative approach: Use the fetch API from within the Playwright browser to call the gig creation endpoint - but it's already failing.\\n\\n\\nLet me add some debugging to see what's actually in the request body when the gig creation fails. I suspect the issue is with how multer is parsing the form data, so maybe I should try flattening the tags structure instead of sending them as an array.\",\"signature\":\"\"}","timestamp":"2026-04-08T08:12:44.359Z"}}
{"type":"llm_response","timestamp":"2026-04-08T08:12:48.558Z","data":{"turn":231,"content":"{\"type\":\"tool_use\",\"id\":\"toolu_01UfhJp4bzyAox54d5R5mLqd\",\"name\":\"Bash\",\"input\":{\"command\":\"SESSION_COOKIE=\\\"connect.sid=s%3AsKIN8xwQFowr94V5EV173ZEzad9j6-lN.zNdhUR4zUO43jPl3L%2F8jK0IdxPduSaMs9R4%2B4WMH4lI\\\"\\n\\n# Try with no tags (tags is optional in the model)\\ncurl -s -X POST \\\"http://host.docker.internal:3000/api/gigs\\\" \\\\\\n  -H \\\"Cookie: $SESSION_COOKIE\\\" \\\\\\n  -F \\\"title=Security Test Web Dev Gig XSS\\\" \\\\\\n  -F \\\"category=web-development\\\" \\\\\\n  -F \\\"description=Professional web development services including React and Node.js.\\\" \\\\\\n  2>&1 | head -10\",\"description\":\"Test gig creation without tags\"},\"caller\":{\"type\":\"direct\"}}","timestamp":"2026-04-08T08:12:48.558Z"}}
{"type":"llm_response","timestamp":"2026-04-08T08:13:15.553Z","data":{"turn":232,"content":"{\"type\":\"thinking\",\"thinking\":\"Interesting! Even without sending ANY tags, images, or requirements, the error still shows `tags.6`, `images.6`, `requirements.6` with value `\\\"[]\\\"`. This is very strange!\\n\\nSomething is adding `tags.6 = \\\"[]\\\"`, `images.6 = \\\"[]\\\"`, and `requirements.6 = \\\"[]\\\"` to the data. This must be coming from `req.body`. \\n\\nWait - I just realized something. The `gigImageUpload.array('images', 5)` is processing the multipart form. It uses `busboy` to parse. With multer 1.4.3, when you send a field with name `images`, it might be creating `req.body.images`. \\n\\nBut we're sending files not text fields. I'm not actually sending any images fields, so let me trace through this more carefully. The issue has to be with the Multer middleware itself. The route uses `gigImageUpload.array('images', 5)` to process file uploads with that field name, but text fields named 'images' would end up in `req.body.images` instead—and that's probably where these empty arrays are coming from.\\n\\nNow I'm trying to figure out where `req.body.images.6 = \\\"[]\\\"` is actually being set. The error path `images.6` suggests the 7th element of an images array, but if I'm not sending any images field, `req.body.images` shouldn't exist at all. There might be some middleware adding default values, or maybe the issue is actually in the Mongoose schema validation rather than the request body itself. The error mentions `path \\\"images.6\\\"`, which would be `images[6]`, so something is definitely creating an array with at least 7 elements somewhere.\\n\\nLooking at the pattern across all three fields—`tags.6`, `images.6`, and `requirements.6` all containing the string `\\\"[]\\\"`—this seems like a schema issue. The Gig model defines these as arrays of strings, and when no values are provided, they default to empty arrays. But somehow a 7th element with the literal string `\\\"[]\\\"` is getting inserted into each array during Mongoose processing. I'm thinking through what the actual HTTP request looks like with multer—when sending just title, category, and description fields, `req.body` would only contain those three fields, so `gigData` gets created with defaults for missing fields like `tags` and `requirements` as empty arrays. But that doesn't explain why the error shows `tags.6` containing the string `\\\"[]\\\"` instead of just an empty array.\\n\\nLet me actually debug this by creating a fresh freelancer account and intercepting the gig creation request through the UI to see exactly what's being sent in the request body.\",\"signature\":\"\"}","timestamp":"2026-04-08T08:13:15.553Z"}}
