{"type":"llm_response","timestamp":"2026-04-08T08:13:40.023Z","data":{"turn":237,"content":"{\"type\":\"thinking\",\"thinking\":\"I see that `express.urlencoded({ extended: true })` is used. This means URL-encoded bodies are parsed with the `qs` library (extended mode). The `qs` library supports nested objects and arrays.\\n\\nBut for multipart form data (which is what we're sending with `-F`), this middleware doesn't apply. Multer handles multipart form data.\\n\\nWait, actually I wonder if the issue is this: `express.urlencoded({ extended: true })` is ALSO processing the multipart form? Let me check - no, `urlencoded` only handles `application/x-www-form-urlencoded`, not `multipart/form-data`. Multer handles `multipart/form-data`.\\n \\nI could try debugging by adding a temporary route to inspect what's actually in the request body or check what got stored in the database. But honestly, the code analysis is already conclusive: `dangerouslySetInnerHTML` is rendering the review comment without any sanitization at `GigDetail.jsx:299`, the XSS payload flows through the API unescaped, and there's no CSP to block execution.\\n\\nThe real blocker is that there are no gigs in MongoDB to test against. Let me pivot and create a gig using my test freelancer account instead, then post a malicious review and visit that gig page to confirm the XSS fires. I have a valid contract with freelancer `d9d272cc-4ab8-4ffc-a60f-93108836bed4`, but I need to work with my own test account credentials to actually create and test this end-to-end.\",\"signature\":\"\"}","timestamp":"2026-04-08T08:13:40.023Z"}}
