{"type":"llm_response","timestamp":"2026-04-08T08:28:39.792Z","data":{"turn":298,"content":"{\"type\":\"tool_use\",\"id\":\"toolu_016e5qKp2zZb8sije4CNNXL7\",\"name\":\"Bash\",\"input\":{\"command\":\"# Also verify what headers the server returns for the HTML file\\ncurl -I \\\"http://host.docker.internal:3000/uploads/555a7fd7-9b05-4a77-b1b5-f66a90d3c7d6.html\\\"\",\"description\":\"Check Content-Type and security headers for uploaded HTML file\"},\"caller\":{\"type\":\"direct\"}}","timestamp":"2026-04-08T08:28:39.792Z"}}
{"type":"llm_response","timestamp":"2026-04-08T08:28:46.776Z","data":{"turn":299,"content":"{\"type\":\"thinking\",\"thinking\":\"Key findings from the headers:\\n1. `Content-Type: text/html; charset=UTF-8` — The file is served as HTML, not as an attachment\\n2. `X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff` — Present, but it doesn't help here because the file is genuinely served as `text/html`\\n3. **No Content-Security-Policy header** — CSP explicitly disabled in the server config\\n4. `X-XSS-Protection: 0` — XSS auditor disabled\\n\\nThis confirms the file upload XSS vector is fully exploitable:\\n\\n\\nThe HTML file retains its extension when uploaded, gets served with the proper HTML content type, and there's nothing preventing the JavaScript from executing directly in the application's origin context. Now I'm checking whether SVG uploads could also be leveraged for the same attack, since they're another vector for script execution.\",\"signature\":\"\"}","timestamp":"2026-04-08T08:28:46.776Z"}}
{"type":"llm_response","timestamp":"2026-04-08T08:28:47.202Z","data":{"turn":300,"content":"Excellent! `Content-Type: text/html; charset=UTF-8` confirmed — no `X-Content-Type-Options` override issue here since the file genuinely is HTML. No CSP header. **XSS-VULN-02 confirmed.**\n\nNow let me also test an SVG upload and then move to the Puppeteer injection analysis:","timestamp":"2026-04-08T08:28:47.202Z"}}
