{"type":"llm_response","timestamp":"2026-04-08T08:31:13.736Z","data":{"turn":327,"content":"{\"type\":\"thinking\",\"thinking\":\"Both `Conversation.jsx:238` (`msg.attachment_url`) and `ContractDetail.jsx:116` (`milestone.deliverable_url`) use `href={...}` with user-controlled data. These are potential `javascript:` URL injection vectors.\\n\\nLet me check:\\n1. **`msg.attachment_url`**: This comes from the messages API. Looking at the messaging service, messages store `attachments` as a JSON array with `path`, `size`, `filename`, `mimetype` fields. But in `Conversation.jsx`, it references `msg.attachment_url` and `msg.attachment_name` — these fields don't come directly from the database `attachments` array. \\n\\nThe frontend likely maps `attachments[0].path` to `attachment_url`, or this could be dead code.\\n\\n2. **`milestone.deliverable_url`**: Checking how milestones are returned — `deliverable_url` doesn't appear to be a database field. From the submit deliverable function, deliverables are stored as JSON in `milestone.deliverables`, not as a separate `deliverable_url` field. So this is probably dead code too.\\n\\nLet me trace through the Conversation component to see how `attachment_url` actually gets populated.\",\"signature\":\"\"}","timestamp":"2026-04-08T08:31:13.736Z"}}
