{"type":"llm_response","timestamp":"2026-04-08T08:31:44.441Z","data":{"turn":331,"content":"{\"type\":\"thinking\",\"thinking\":\"I notice at line 96: `body.append('file', attachment);` — the frontend is using the field name `file` when uploading. But earlier when I tested with field name `attachments`, it worked. Let me check the messaging routes more carefully.\\n\\nAlso, `msg.attachment_url` — this field doesn't appear to be set anywhere in the loaded data. Looking at line 40 (`setMessages(data.messages || [])`), the messages are loaded from the API which returns `attachments` as an array like `[{\\\"path\\\":\\\"/uploads/...\\\", \\\"size\\\":..., \\\"filename\\\":..., \\\"mimetype\\\":...}]`. \\n\\nSo the rendering checks `msg.attachment_url`, which doesn't exist in that structure. This looks like dead code — the `href={msg.attachment_url}` sink in Conversation.jsx would never be exploitable through the normal API path since that field is never populated. But the WebSocket message handler at line 58-68 might receive messages with a different structure, so I need to check what the `new_message` event actually emits from the messaging service.\\n\\nChecking messaging.service.js...\\n\\nThe socket emits `{ message: newMessage, conversation_id: ... }` but the component is checking `msg.conversation_id` and `msg.id` directly. There's a mismatch here — the actual message object is nested under `msg.message`, so `msg.id` wouldn't exist unless the handler is flattening the structure somehow. When these socket messages get rendered, the properties don't align — `msg.content` and `msg.attachment_url` would both be undefined since the actual data is nested under `msg.message`. The API endpoint returns messages with an `attachments` array, so `attachment_url` looks like dead code referencing a field that doesn't exist. The real issue is that socket messages have a different structure than API responses, causing a mismatch in how they're accessed downstream.\\n\\nNow I'm checking whether the milestones API returns a `deliverable_url` field or something else.\",\"signature\":\"\"}","timestamp":"2026-04-08T08:31:44.441Z"}}
