{"type":"llm_response","timestamp":"2026-04-08T08:32:32.087Z","data":{"turn":340,"content":"{\"type\":\"thinking\",\"thinking\":\"All other text renderings (`bio`, `description`, `display_name`, etc.) are rendered via JSX expressions `{...}` which auto-escape HTML. Only `review.comment` uses `dangerouslySetInnerHTML`.\\n\\nNow let me quickly test the `display_name` in Puppeteer PDF injection to see if it actually executes JS. But first, let me reconsider the approach:\\n\\nFor the Puppeteer injection:\\n- The HTML template at line 61 interpolates `${data.clientName}` (which is `client.display_name`)\\n\\n\\n- If I set my `display_name` to `<script>...</script>`, it gets embedded directly into the HTML that Puppeteer renders\\n- Since Puppeteer runs `page.setContent(html)` server-side with `waitUntil: 'networkidle0'`, this is server-side headless browser injection rather than traditional XSS\\n- I should classify this as \\\"Server-Side HTML Injection via Puppeteer\\\" and check whether there's an SSRF angle to exploit\\n\\nNow I'm tracing through the actual data flow: the user's malicious `display_name` flows unescaped into the invoice HTML template, gets rendered by Puppeteer on the server, and any scripts execute in that headless browser context. This gives me enough to assess the real impact—it's server-side XSS with potential SSRF capabilities since Puppeteer can make outbound requests, and it definitely allows PDF content manipulation. I should flag this as high-severity for the exploitation queue.\\n\\nLet me verify the contracts endpoint is actually reachable and trace through the controller to confirm the PDF generation path is exploitable.\",\"signature\":\"\"}","timestamp":"2026-04-08T08:32:32.087Z"}}
