{"type":"llm_response","timestamp":"2026-04-08T08:33:36.535Z","data":{"turn":353,"content":"{\"type\":\"thinking\",\"thinking\":\"The `display_name` was updated to contain HTML injection! The API accepted the payload without any sanitization. Now I need to:\\n1. Get a contract ID where `xsstest2` is the client\\n2. Request the invoice for that contract\\n3. Observe whether Puppeteer executes the injected JavaScript\\n\\nBut to have a contract with `xsstest2` as the client, I need to:\\n1. Find an existing contract where xsstest2 is involved, OR\\n2. Create a new contract\\n\\nLet me check if there are existing contracts I can use, or if I can quickly create one. \\n\\nI realize the `generateInvoice` function doesn't validate contract ownership, so any authenticated user can request any contract's invoice. But the HTML injection targets `client.display_name`, so I need `xsstest2` to actually be the client on a contract for the payload to execute. I could either find an existing contract and update that client's display_name, or create a new contract with `xsstest2` as the client.\\n\\nSince I can only modify my own profile, I need to create a contract where `xsstest2` is the client. Looking back at the recon findings, there's an IDOR vulnerability on contract creation that might let me bypass the normal flow of needing a gig, proposal, and acceptance.\",\"signature\":\"\"}","timestamp":"2026-04-08T08:33:36.535Z"}}
